**Read:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), [section 6.3.3.1, count charts](https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc331.htm). Revisit the aerosol counting and background models.

A Poisson count model assumes a defined exposure and a suitably stable independent-event rate. Its mean and variance are both λ. A detected particle count, a number of defect sites and a count of nonconforming products are different variables; one product may contain several sites.

At λ = 1, the probability of observing at least 5 counts is about 0.003659847, while at least 6 has probability about 0.000594185. There is no integer threshold producing every desired tail probability exactly. Symmetric mean ± 3√mean limits do not inherit normal-tail probabilities for sparse counts.

Zero counts remain an observation with finite exposure and detection limits. Background subtraction can yield a noninteger estimate with a different variance; it cannot automatically be fed into an ordinary count chart as a raw Poisson count.

**Work:** Reproduce exact Poisson tails and compare a normal approximation across several means. Reconstruct the handbook's wafer example, then distinguish the count statistic from the decision that a wafer is functionally unacceptable.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
